{"schema_version":1,"objective_id":"QFORM-FIVE-SINGULAR-VALUES-LINE-AUDIT-20260925","source_commit":"ec211ba2d77a4a9850168c6af1574ea651feaa7c","source_blob":"c4f2a997cf336ee974e15409b5bccdd8cca039d8","pdf_sha256":"4c1a8eb808b32739625aa475c1aad24dc857a3f35fc960504b371487f2850423","account":"fleet02","slot":"B10","run_id":"fleet02-B10-20260925T011508Z-audit-pass3","pass_id":"pass3_exact_constants_adversarial","statement_label":"S3 Projections","tex_line_start":195,"tex_line_end":195,"clause_id":"L195","clause_excerpt":"We justify $\\frac{\\dd}{\\dd\\tau}\\int F\\dd x=\\int\\partial_\\tau F\\dd x\\ge\\int\\Delta_xF\\dd x=0$ on a compact interval $\\tau\\in[\\tau_0,\\tau_1]\\subset(0,\\infty)$. There $f_i(\\tau,y)\\le2m\\norm{h_i}_\\infty\\sup_{\\abs z\\le m}g_\\tau(y-z)\\le Ce^{-y^2/(16\\tau_1)}$ (for $\\abs y\\ge2m$ use $\\abs{y-z}\\ge\\abs y/2$), and $\\partial_y\\log f_i$ is the average of $-(y-z)/(2\\tau)$ over $z\\in[-m,m]$ against a probability measure, so $\\abs{\\partial_y\\log f_i(\\tau,y)}\\le(\\abs y+m)/(2\\tau_0)$; similar bounds hold for the second derivative. Using $\\sum_ic_i(u_i\\cdot x)^2=\\abs x^2$, the functions $F$, $\\partial_\\tau F$ and the first two $x$-derivatives of $F$ are bounded by $C(1+\\abs x^4)e^{-\\abs x^2/(16\\tau_1)}$ uniformly in $\\tau\\in[\\tau_0,\\tau_1]$. This justifies differentiating under the integral sign and shows $\\int\\Delta_xF\\dd x=0$. Thus $\\int F(\\tau,x)\\dd x$ is nondecreasing in $\\tau$.","pdf_page":"4","status":"PASS","hypotheses_used":"Geometric frame and bounded compact test functions, then monotone truncation.","dependencies":"Heat equation, integration by parts, frame identity.","derivation_or_objection":"Heat-flow monotonicity sign, domination, tau→0/infinity limits independently checked.","checks_performed":"fresh pass3 direct derivation","downstream_impact":"Feeds product projections/crossing decay.","repair_reference":null,"independence_note":"Fresh pass3."}
